  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 416 AM MDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nWeakening showers were slow to exit the northeast Colorado plains\nin the last couple of hours, with estimated totals in that area \ngenerally ranging from 0.5\"-2.0\", and perhaps a few spots \nreceiving as much as 3.0\" of rain. Given the antecedent rainfall \nand anomalous moisture expected once again this afternoon and \nevening, we have decided to issue a Flood Watch for flash flooding\nextending from eastern Weld and Logan County, down to parts of \nElbert and Lincoln counties.\n\nAs previous AFDs have highlighted, precipitable water (PW) values \nfor today continue to be 150%-200% of the climatological average for \nthis time of year, in the 1.3\"-1.6\" range for the low elevations and \nas high as 1.0\" over the mountains. Moisture this anomalous combined \nwith weak storm motion vectors (~15KT) and a relatively deep warm \ncloud layer (5,000 ft) indicate a flash flood threat with the \nstrongest storms, particularly with training or redevelopment over \nthe same areas. Additionally, the lower levels of the atmosphere are \nmuch moister than they were yesterday afternoon meaning that \nrainfall production will be more efficient from the start. The \nmain weakness or failure mode for today's shower setup would be \nlimited instability over the I-25 corridor and Denver metro given\nthe cooler temperatures and increased cloud cover. However, high-\nres models do at least show some pockets of MLCAPE >1500 J/kg. \nEven if showers are initially isolated in nature, any outflow \nboundaries produced by those first few showers and storms will be\nable to kickstart additional shower activity. For the high \nterrain, there will not be many obstacles in the way of shower and\nthunderstorm development as showers are already moving into the \nGore Range and central Colorado mountains.\n\nFor Friday and Saturday, PWs decrease slightly but values stay at or \nabove the 90th percentile of climatology. Scattered showers and \nstorms will be possible each afternoon and localized flash \nflooding will still be a threat, particularly with storms tracking\nover areas that received rainfall the previous day. Looking at \nthe synoptic pattern for the weekend, global models have begun to \nsuggest a weakening/widening of the upper level ridge over the \ncentral U.S. as well as a weakening of the trough off the Pacific \ncoast. While this change would dilute monsoonal moisture \ntransport, flow aloft over the region will remain southwesterly \nand moisture will not be cutoff completely. Showers and storms \nwill be possible each afternoon, although the spatial coverage may\nnot be as widespread.\n\nMonday and Tuesday appear to be drier as flow aloft becomes more \nzonal due to the upper level ridge axis temporarily retreating \nwestwards over the Four Corners. Long-range ensemble guidance \ncontinues to suggest moisture returning to the region by the \nmiddle of next week, as well as the passage of a few shortwaves. A\nwarming trend is still on track to begin on Monday, with \ntemperatures in the 90s likely returning by Tuesday afternoon for \nthe low elevations.",
